{"name":"Chinchilla Chenille","hex":"#d0bba7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"chinchilla","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small, crepuscular rodent of the genus Chinchilla, native to the Andes Mountains, prized for their very soft fur and often kept as pets.","The fur of a chinchilla, used for clothing."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chinchilla"},{"term":"chenille","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An extremely soft and bunchy fabric often used to make sweaters."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chenille"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chinchilla","blocks":[{"text":"Chinchilla is a genus consisting of two species of crepuscular rodents of the parvorder Caviomorpha, native to the Andes Mountains in South America. They live in colonies called \"herds\" at high elevations up to 4,270 m (14,000 ft). Historically, chinchillas lived in an area that included parts of Bolivia, Peru and Chile, but today, colonies in the wild are known only in Chile. Along with their relatives, viscachas, they make up the family Chinchillidae. They are also related to the chinchilla rat."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Chinchilla_lanigera_%28Wroclaw_zoo%29-2.JPG/330px-Chinchilla_lanigera_%28Wroclaw_zoo%29-2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinchilla","wikidata":"Q192930"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Habitat_du_Chinchilla_lanigera_-_Auco.jpg/500px-Habitat_du_Chinchilla_lanigera_-_Auco.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Habitat_du_Chinchilla_lanigera_-_Auco.jpg/500px-Habitat_du_Chinchilla_lanigera_-_Auco.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Chinchilla habitat in the Andes Mountains of Chile","link":"ht